00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by dr.  mona hanna-attisha who is a pediatrician  in Flint Michigan associate professor of  pediatrics at Michigan State University  she was key in exposing the dangerous  levels of lead in Flint Michigan's  drinking water which is a story that  we've been covering for a long time  sadly it the story is not over and we're  going to talk about that in part her new  book is titled what the eyes don't see a  story of crisis resistance and hope in  an American city so let's let's go to  the very beginning if we can how did  this issue of Flint's water problem get  on your radar so it happened very much  by serendipity as a pediatrician I take  care of the children in Flint and moms  were coming in and asking about the  water it was the water safe to use to  bathe my baby was the water safe to use  to mix my baby's formula with us water  and I was very much reassuring my  patients yeah the water's fine  who wouldn't expect in America in the  21st century in the middle of the Great  Lakes for the water that comes out of  your tap not to be okay so I was very  much drinking the kool-aid from the  state reassuring the people that I was  taking care of that the water was okay  so that all changed actually at my home  with a high school girlfriend who  happened to be over for a last minute  barbecue and my girlfriend as a water  expert and she informed me that the  water was not fine it was not safe to  drink and that it wasn't being treated  properly and without the important  ingredient called corrosion control  there was going to be lead in the water  and that was the very first time that I  heard about the possibility of lead a  potent irreversible neurotoxin into our  water supply now when the parents were  coming in and asking you about the  safety of the water was this just  generally something that concerned  parents asked about or was there a  particular reason that the parents were  asking you about this in Flint Michigan  yeah absolutely so the the water switch  happened in April of 2014 and I didn't  get involved until about 17 months late  and throughout this period the people of  Flint were brave and heroic and loud and  organized and they knew something was  wrong with the water so right away the  water looked brown it tasted weird  we had bacteria in the water but then it  was treated with a lot of disinfectant  so they all clear was made and then we  had so much disinfectant which was  chlorine and irritated people's skin and  eyes and then they fixed that so there  were multiple problems with this water  source but with each problem the state  was reassuring the citizens that  everything was okay but really they were  all missed opportunities I think the the  biggest miss opportunity was just a  several several months into the water  switch General Motors which was born in  Flint stopped using this water cuz it  was corroding engine parts imagine that  our water was corroding engine parts but  the people of Flint were being told to  relax that it was okay so I had blinders  on just like many folks in Flint had  blinders on and that's why the title of  this book is what the eyes don't see  because you can't see lead in water and  it's if you can't see the problems with  lead but fundamentally it's about people  and places and problems all over in  Flint and beyond that we choose not to  see and it is our duty to open our eyes  and to be aware of these problems once  you finally realize what was going on  and started making noise about it can  you talk a little bit about what  strategies were used to either try to  discredit the meat of what you were  saying or merely to stop the word from  getting out yeah so you know like I  mentioned the people of Flint were  raising concerns and they were dismissed  so the Flint water crisis is a story of  a denial of democracy denial of the  voice of citizens and very much the  denial of science so I talked about that  basic science of how the engine parts  work voting that was denied everybody  who tried to raise a concern was  discredited and dismissed from the moms  and the activists and the pastors and  the journalists and then the water  scientists came in and said hey look we  have lead in the water this water is 19  times more corrosive than the Great  Lakes water that we had been getting and  though  water scientists were being dismissed so  when I brought forth the data the  research the science that I was  impacting our children and it was  getting in their blood I was also  discredited so Flint is another example  of where science was denied and we see  this nationally happening right now  where clear-cut science on things like  climate change and vaccines are also  being denied so the strategies that we  invoked is is fighting back with more  science you know we every number every  every evidence every fact in my research  was not a number it was a child it was a  childhood I'd probably cared for so we  really fought back with with more  science and and ultimately at that point  the media started to become an important  player in the uncovering of the story  and helped really finally turn the tide  and the state conceded why is lead bad  for the body I mean now we know this for  a long time we didn't and in fact lead  was used in the production of all sorts  of different goods that humans came in  contact with all the time but what are  the health risks that's a great question  David so we've been using lead for  centuries and actually we've known about  its badness for for centuries the Romans  first put lead in there aqueducts the  word lead actually comes from the Latin  plumbum the elemental symbol for lettuce  TB it was so malleable so even back then  many people hypothesize the demise of  Romans was because they used so much  lead not only in plumbing but they also  added it to their food which was  terrible but we put LED in gasoline  because there was a profit to be made  we put lead and paint we have greedily  corporations have put lead in a lot of  industrial goods that have continued to  poison people for generations now and to  come when pediatricians tear the word  lead we really freak out because we know  what it does to children it is potent it  is irreversible  it is a neurotoxin it fundamentally  attacks the everything of what a child  is and does it impacts cognition how  children think dropping IQ levels it  impacts behavior causes the Bell  mental developmental delays and it's  even been linked to criminalities so  levels that we thought were okay decades  ago incredible science has now taught us  that there is no safe level of lead in a  child so yes it's been great that  people's lead levels have been dropping  over the last few decades as we got let  out of paint and gasoline but we should  be paying more attention because we now  know there is no safe level and we also  know that the lowest levels can actually  cause considerable decline in children's  cognition and behavior and what's most  striking is that lead as a form of  environmental injustice a form of  environmental racism our kids in Flint  already had higher blood levels just  like kids in Detroit and Chicago in  Baltimore and some of these cities with  our most vulnerable children are already  disproportionately exposed to lead we  sometimes in corporate media we see that  stories go away after they've sort of  run their course but that doesn't  necessarily mean the problem has been  solved we saw this for example in the  aftermath of the hurricanes in Puerto  Rico which is a really notable example  just because we're not hearing as much  about what's going on in Flint at this  point in corporate media is everything  fine now with the water there  no and that's absolutely why I wrote  this book when I started this two and a  half years ago I knew that we would need  a way to shine a spotlight back on Flint  because our story is nowhere near over  our we had 18 months of corrosive water  that ran through our system that ate off  our pipes those are being replaced so  that's that's great news but until those  are replaced people still are on  filtered water and bottled water but  where I spend most of my time is in  terms of the recovery of the children we  can't take away this crisis but we are  very much and very much what this book  hopes to share is a story of resistance  of activism and of hope so science was  denied in Flint science ultimately spoke  truth to power and we are using the  incredible science of child development  and resilience to buffer the impact of  this crisis and that is that is hope and  that is what we're hoping to share so we  have things in place in Flint that  aren't being done anywhere else in this  country for example child care services  and home visiting programs and universal  preschool and early literacy support a  Medicaid expansion so but these are  things that all children need everywhere  so we are leveraging this crisis to make  sure that our children who did nothing  wrong besides live in a city that was  almost bankrupt lost democracy and  didn't treat its water properly do not  see the consequences of this crisis so  so that is what is happening Flint so  Flint is really a model of of a crisis  but really a model of how we can turn  this crisis around and really serve as  an example to other communities where  children are suffering from very similar  toxicities via the toxicity of poverty  or violence or lost democracy the book  is great but it is about a very very  dark chapter in American history I mean  I don't think that that that's going too  far to say that the book is called what  the eyes don't see a story of crisis  resistance and hope in an American city  we've been speaking with the book's  author dr. mona hanna-attisha thank you  so much and and thank you so much for  exposing this because it is it could  have easily been covered up for much  longer and we're all it's horrible but  we're all better off that it was exposed  when it did and not a year or two later  it's true  thank you

